//? Title: Data Augmentation: Transform Images and Targets Together
//? Subtitle: CutMix labels follow clipped area; mixup labels follow the same interpolation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A training transformation can silently assign the wrong target to an altered image.
We inspect flips, crops and two image-mixing schemes on small NHWC arrays. Exact pixel checks
and a sweep of mixing weights distinguish spatial changes from target interpolation;
no improvement in classification accuracy is inferred.
)
\toc

> The method

A horizontal flip maps x[n,y,z,c] to x[n,y,W-1-z,c], so applying it twice is the identity.
A crop selects an explicit spatial window. These transformations change the input while
preserving a class label only when the task's semantics justify that invariance.
Mixup and CutMix instead construct soft targets:

$$
\widetilde x=\lambda x+(1-\lambda)x_\pi,\quad
\widetilde y=\lambda y+(1-\lambda)y_\pi,\qquad
\lambda_{\rm CutMix}=1-\frac{|R\cap[0,H)\times[0,W)|}{HW}.
$$
\alttext(Mixup interpolates both images and labels with one coefficient. CutMix uses the fraction of image area retained after clipping its rectangle.)

For CutMix the pixels inside R come from the partner; it is the clipped, realised area
that sets the target coefficient. Valid label rows retain unit mass because the coefficients
are nonnegative and sum to one. Explicit Generator objects make random calls repeatable;
evaluation bypasses augmentation. These preprocessing functions return new values without
recording an autograd graph. Each transform takes O(NHWC) work.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/data_augmentation_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/data_augmentation_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/data_augmentation_concept.tikz > build/data_augmentation_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/data_augmentation_concept.svg build/data_augmentation_concept.dvi > build/data_augmentation_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/data_augmentation_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/data_augmentation_concept.svg from local TikZ source.
