> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
 namespace n=dm::nn;namespace a=n::augment;
 const auto x=n::reshape(n::arange(0,32),{2,4,4,1})/32., y=n::tensor({{1,0},{0,1}});
 double worst=0;
 for(int i=0;i<=20;++i){const auto m=a::mixup(x,y,{1,0},i/20.);worst=std::max(worst,n::max(n::abs(n::sum(m.labels,{1})-1)).item());}
 const double flip=n::max(n::abs(a::flip_left_right(a::flip_left_right(x))-x)).item();
 const auto crop=a::crop(x,1,1,2,2);
 n::Generator g(7),repeat(7);double rng_error=0;
 for(int i=0;i<20;++i)rng_error=std::max(rng_error,std::abs(a::beta_mix_weight(g,.4)-a::beta_mix_weight(repeat,.4)));
 int rejected=0;try{a::mixup(x,y,{1,1},.5);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
 try{a::mixup(x,y,{1,0},1.5);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
 if(worst>1e-6||flip!=0||rng_error!=0||rejected!=2||crop.at({0,0,0,0})!=5/32.)return 1;
 std::print("21 mixing weights: maximum label-mass error {:.9f}.\n\nDouble-flip error: {}; crop first value: {}; seeded Beta replay error: {}; invalid inputs rejected: {}.\n",worst,flip,crop.at({0,0,0,0}),rng_error,rejected);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
21 mixing weights: maximum label-mass error 0.000000000.

Double-flip error: 0; crop first value: 0.15625; seeded Beta replay error: 0; invalid inputs rejected: 2.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The spatial and label checks agree with the construction at boundaries and across mixing weights.
Area clipping is essential to CutMix's target. Reproducibility of transformations is useful for
validation, but task-specific invariance and a separate held-out evaluation determine whether
augmentation helps a model.

> References

- [Zhang et al. (2018), mixup: Beyond Empirical Risk Minimization](https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09412).
- [Yun et al. (2019), CutMix](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.04899).
